 \begin{abstract}
-In this paper, we develop an integrated mathematical and computational framework for analyzing and forecasting web-framework activity using two complementary open-source signals: monthly npm package downloads and GitHub repository star increments. We introduce a geometric composite dynamics index and establish its mathematical properties, including reference-period invariance of logarithmic changes and a criterion for the stability of the aggregate direction under variations in component weights. The framework combines discrete-time stochastic modeling with rolling-origin evaluation of SARIMA, ETS, and Prophet models. We compare direct index forecasting with component-wise forecasting and construct horizon-specific prediction intervals for the index using paired residual bootstrap simulation. To support computational transparency and reproducibility, we implement a computational system that validates the monthly observations and links each published result to its saved numerical output. An empirical study of React, Angular, and Vue demonstrates that the component signals may exhibit opposing temporal trends, highlighting the importance of weight-sensitivity analysis when interpreting composite dynamics. The forecasting results also reveal differences in model performance across indicators, with SARIMA providing the lowest twelve-month root mean squared error for all three download series. For the equal-weight index, component forecasts achieved the lowest weighted absolute percentage error in seven of twelve framework--horizon comparisons.
+This paper presents a computational framework for analyzing and forecasting web-framework activity from monthly npm package downloads and GitHub repository star increments. The two counts are modeled as a bivariate discrete-time stochastic process, from which we define a geometric composite dynamics index. We establish measurability, integrability, almost sure invariance of logarithmic changes to the reference normalization, and a criterion for directional stability under changes in component weights. Rolling-origin evaluation compares SARIMA, ETS and Prophet with ordinary naive (Naive) and seasonal naive (SNaive) benchmarks. We compare direct index forecasts with forecasts assembled from components, and construct horizon-specific prediction intervals using paired residual bootstrap simulation. The associated predictive law also defines a conditional probability of directional stability. A computational system validates monthly observations and links published results to saved numerical outputs. For React, Angular and Vue, the component signals have opposing year-on-year directions in 94 of 108 framework--month observations, demonstrating the relevance of weight-sensitivity analysis. SARIMA has the lowest twelve-month root mean squared error for all three download series. For the equal-weight index, component forecasts attain the lowest weighted absolute percentage error in seven of twelve framework--horizon comparisons. Persistence benchmarks remain competitive, and low point error does not ensure informative prediction intervals.
 \end{abstract}
