+\subsection{The dynamics index as a stochastic process}\label{sec:index}
+For a fixed framework, let $D_t$ and $S_t$ denote the monthly downloads and the monthly reconstructed star increments, and consider the bivariate process $\mathbf X_t=(D_t,S_t)^{\top}:\Omega\to\mathbb N_0^{2}$, $t\in\mathcal T$, on the probability space of Section~\ref{sec:setting}. For $X\in\{D,S\}$ let $y^{X}_t=\log(1+X_t)$, $\mathcal F^{X}_t=\sigma(X_u:u\le t)$, and let $\mathcal G_t=\mathcal F^{D}_t\vee\mathcal F^{S}_t$ be the joint filtration. Neither independence of the coordinates nor stationarity is assumed; the two signals may be dependent contemporaneously and across time. Besides the second-moment condition of Section~\ref{sec:setting}, we assume $\mathbb E[X_t]<\infty$. The panel September 2018--August 2026 is a realization over 96 months. Proofs are given in Supplementary Section~S1.
+
+\subsubsection{Definition and almost sure properties}
+Let $B$ be the fixed reference period September 2022--August 2023 and $t_B=\max B$. These are the twelve complete calendar months immediately preceding the first origin month, September 2023. All six component series cover this period. Using a full annual cycle includes each calendar month once, while averaging over twelve observations avoids anchoring the scale to a single month. The common dates align the comparison across frameworks; the component means remain framework-specific. The reference is held fixed at all 24 origins and is available before each training cutoff, so its computation uses no target observations. This is a transparent normalization convention chosen retrospectively for the exploratory study, not a preregistered or statistically optimal baseline. It does not remove seasonal variation or make the reference year representative of normal activity: the December 2022 Vue peak remains in its mean (Section~\ref{sec:limits}).
+
+ The reference levels $\mu_X=|B|^{-1}\sum_{t\in B}\mathbb E[X_t]$ are deterministic, whereas their empirical counterparts $\overline X_B=|B|^{-1}\sum_{t\in B}X_t$ are $\mathcal G_{t_B}$-measurable random variables; $C_X=\log(1+\overline X_B)$ is a random normalization constant.
+
+\begin{definition}[Stochastic geometric dynamics index]\label{def:index}
+For $t\in\mathcal T$, $X\in\{D,S\}$ and a download weight $w\in[0,1]$, let $R_{X,t}=(X_t+1)/(\overline X_B+1)$, $U_{X,t}=\log R_{X,t}=y^{X}_t-C_X$, and
+\begin{equation}
+I_t(w)=100\,R_{D,t}^{\,w}R_{S,t}^{\,1-w},\qquad Z_t(w)=\log\frac{I_t(w)}{100}=wU_{D,t}+(1-w)U_{S,t}.
+\label{eq:idx-def}
+\end{equation}
+The \emph{theoretical index} $I^{0}_t(w)$ is defined by the same formulas with $\overline X_B$ replaced by $\mu_X$.
 \end{definition}
 
