+\subsubsection{Joint predictive distribution and bootstrap approximation}\label{sec:idx-bootstrap}
+Both the predictive law of $I_{T+h}(w)$ and $\operatorname{DSP}_{T+h\mid T}(a)$ are functionals of the conditional joint law of $(U_{D,T+h},U_{S,T+h})$ given $\mathcal G_T$, which by Proposition~\ref{prop:idx-properties}(v) is not determined by the two marginals. We approximate it by a paired residual bootstrap in the spirit of~\cite{thombs,pascual}. For each family and origin $T=T_o$, the innovation residuals of the two fitted component models are centered after discarding the first 12; months $\tau_1,\dots,\tau_h$ are drawn independently and uniformly from the retained training months, and the residuals of \emph{both} components at $\tau_i$ are used as the innovations at $T+i$, which preserves their empirical contemporaneous dependence. The simulation uses the fitted SARIMA, ETS and baseline innovation responses, with parameters fixed and paths centered on the saved point forecasts; Prophet adds them to its fixed fitted trend and seasonality, excluding future changepoint uncertainty. This yields $(U^{*}_{D,T+h},U^{*}_{S,T+h})$ and $I^{*(b)}_{T+h}(w)=100\exp\{wU^{*}_{D,T+h}+(1-w)U^{*}_{S,T+h}\}$, $b=1,\dots,N$, with $N=4000$; the direct route resamples the residuals of the model for $Z_t(w)$. With $\widehat q_p$ the empirical $p$-quantile of the simulated index, the nominal $(1-\alpha)$ interval, $\alpha\in\{0.20,0.05\}$, and a proposed Monte Carlo estimator of the directional stability probability are
+\begin{equation}
+\operatorname{PI}_{1-\alpha}=\bigl[\widehat q_{\alpha/2},\,\widehat q_{1-\alpha/2}\bigr],\qquad
+\widehat{\operatorname{DSP}}_{T+h\mid T}(a)=\frac1N\sum_{b=1}^{N}\mathbf 1\bigl\{F^{*(b)}_{T+h}(a)\,F^{*(b)}_{T+h}(1-a)>0\bigr\},
+\label{eq:idx-pi}
+\end{equation}
+where $F^{*(b)}_{T+h}(w)=\log\bigl(I^{*(b)}_{T+h}(w)/I_{T+h-12}(w)\bigr)$. Single-month draws are primary; circular three-month blocks~\cite{politis} test sensitivity to short-range residual dependence. Let $\mathbb P^{*}$ denote the bootstrap law given the data. The saved interval experiment uses $w=0.5$. Formula~\eqref{eq:idx-pi} defines a possible extension to directional stability probabilities; such probabilities have not been computed or evaluated in the reported tables. Replicates for different weights must use the same simulated component pair.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Monte Carlo and bootstrap approximation]\label{prop:idx-bootstrap}
+Let $T\ge t_B$, $1\le h\le12$, $w\in[0,1]$ and $0<a<1/2$.
+(i) Given the data, $N\,\widehat{\operatorname{DSP}}_{T+h\mid T}(a)$ is binomial with success probability $\operatorname{DSP}^{*}=\mathbb P^{*}(F^{*}_{T+h}(a)F^{*}_{T+h}(1-a)>0)$, so the standard error of the estimated probability is at most $1/(2\sqrt N)\approx0.008$; as $N\to\infty$, $\widehat q_p$ converges a.s.\ to the bootstrap $p$-quantile if the bootstrap quantile function is continuous at $p$.
+(ii) Suppose that, as the training length grows, the bootstrap law of $(U^{*}_{D,T+h},U^{*}_{S,T+h})$ converges weakly in probability to the conditional law of $(U_{D,T+h},U_{S,T+h})$ given $\mathcal G_T$, and that the latter gives zero mass to the lines $\{wv_D+(1-w)v_S=Z_{T+h-12}(w)\}$, $w\in\{a,1-a\}$. Then $\operatorname{DSP}^{*}\to\operatorname{DSP}_{T+h\mid T}(a)$ in probability. If, in addition, the conditional distribution function of $I_{T+h}(w)$ is continuous and strictly increasing at its quantiles of order $\alpha/2$ and $1-\alpha/2$, the population bootstrap quantiles converge to them in probability. The same conclusion holds for empirical bootstrap endpoints when the number of independent simulation draws also tends to infinity so that their Monte Carlo quantile error vanishes; under these conditions, conditional coverage tends to $1-\alpha$.
+\end{proposition}
+
+Classical bootstrap prediction methods for autoregressive and ARIMA processes motivate this construction~\cite{thombs,pascual}. Proposition~\ref{prop:idx-bootstrap}(ii) is a conditional implication under the stated convergence and continuity assumptions, not a consistency result for our selected models. In particular, continuity at predictive quantiles is a continuous-model idealization, not automatic for integer-valued counts; exact nominal coverage is not generally attainable for discrete laws without randomization. The required assumptions have not been verified for this retrospective experiment, especially for ETS and Prophet. Our intervals omit parameter-estimation, model-selection and Prophet's future changepoint uncertainty; an outer bootstrap that re-estimates the models~\cite{pascual} would include parameter uncertainty at a much higher computational cost. Multiplying marginal limits would not preserve the joint law.
 
