+\clearpage\section*{S1. Proofs for Section~\ref{main-sec:index}}
+Equation, lemma and proposition numbers refer to the main article. Throughout, $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P)$, the filtrations $\mathcal F^{X}_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$, the reference period $B$ with $t_B=\max B$, and the random variables $R_{X,t}$, $U_{X,t}$, $I_t(w)$, $Z_t(w)$, $F_t(w)$ and $W^{*}_t$ are as defined in Section~\ref{main-sec:index}.
+\par\medskip\noindent\textsc{Proof of Lemma~\ref{main-lem:idx-measurability} and Proposition~\ref{main-prop:idx-properties}.}\ 
+$I_t(w)=100\exp\{wU_{D,t}+(1-w)U_{S,t}\}$ is a continuous function of $(D_t,S_t,\overline D_B,\overline S_B)$, which gives Lemma~\ref{main-lem:idx-measurability}(i); the map $w\mapsto Z_t(w,\omega)$ is affine, and $w\mapsto I_t(w,\omega)$ is continuous. Each is measurable in $\omega$ for fixed $w$, so both are Carath\'eodory functions and jointly measurable. On a compact interval their extrema agree with the infimum and supremum over rational points and endpoints; strict sign events for the affine log change are determined by its endpoints. Since $X_u\ge0$, $1\le1+\overline X_B\le\prod_{u\in B}(1+X_u)$, so $0\le C_X\le\sum_{u\in B}y^{X}_u\in L^{2}$ by Minkowski's inequality; the weighted arithmetic--geometric mean (AM--GM) inequality and $R_{X,t}\le X_t+1$ give the bound in Lemma~\ref{main-lem:idx-measurability}(iii). In Proposition~\ref{main-prop:idx-properties}, (i) is the weighted AM--GM inequality, strict for $0<w<1$ and $R_{D,t}\neq R_{S,t}$; (ii) follows by substituting $R_{X,t}=R^{0}_{X,t}(\mu_X+1)/(\overline X_B+1)$ into \eqref{main-eq:idx-def}; in (iii), $K(w)$ cancels and $U_{X,t}-U_{X,t-k}=y^{X}_t-y^{X}_{t-k}$; (iv) is immediate; in (v), the variance is that of a linear combination, the inequality is Jensen's, and for $T\ge t_B$ the $\mathcal G_T$-measurable shift $wC_D+(1-w)C_S$ does not change the conditional variance.\hfill{\setlength{\fboxsep}{0pt}\fbox{\rule{0pt}{1.3ex}\hspace{1.3ex}}}
+\par
+
+\par\medskip\noindent\textsc{Proof of Proposition~\ref{main-prop:idx-stability}.}\ 
+Fix $\omega$ outside the null set on which \eqref{main-eq:idx-annual} fails. (i) $F_t(\cdot,\omega)$ is affine, so on $\mathcal W_a$ it lies between its endpoint values, and its sign is strictly constant iff both are nonzero and of the same sign. (ii) On $\mathcal C_{1,t}$, $F_t$ is a convex combination of two numbers of the same strict sign; on $\mathcal C_{0,t}$ it equals $wG_{D,t}$ or $(1-w)G_{S,t}$, of constant strict sign on $\mathcal W_a$ since $a>0$; on $\mathcal N_t$, $F_t\equiv0$. On $\mathcal A_t$, $F_t$ is strictly monotone with the unique zero $W^{*}_t$, so its sign is constant on $\mathcal W_a$ iff $|W^{*}_t-\frac12|>\frac12-a$; since $|W^{*}_t-\frac12|=(M_t-m_t)/\{2(M_t+m_t)\}$, this is equivalent to $aM_t>(1-a)m_t$. (iii) Formula \eqref{main-eq:idx-dsp} follows from (ii) and disjointness. If $a<a'$, then $\mathcal W_{a'}\subset\mathcal W_a$ and $\mathcal E_t(a)\subseteq\mathcal E_t(a')$. As $a\downarrow0$ the events decrease to $\mathcal C_{1,t}\cup\mathcal C_{0,t}$, because $W^{*}_t\in(0,1)$ on $\mathcal A_t$; as $a\uparrow1/2$ they increase to $\{F_t(1/2)\neq0\}$ by continuity of $F_t$; continuity of $\mathbb P$ along monotone sequences gives the limits. (iv) The same arguments hold for conditional probabilities by conditional monotone convergence; for $t-12\le T$, $y^{X}_{t-12}$ is $\mathcal G_T$-measurable.\hfill{\setlength{\fboxsep}{0pt}\fbox{\rule{0pt}{1.3ex}\hspace{1.3ex}}}
+\par
+
+\par\medskip\noindent\textsc{Proof of Proposition~\ref{main-prop:idx-aggregation}.}\ 
+(i) Linearity of conditional expectation gives the representation; $\widehat Z^{*}_{T+h}$ is the orthogonal projection of $Z_{T+h}(w)$ onto $L^{2}(\Omega,\mathcal G_T,\mathbb P)$, which contains $\widehat Z^{\mathrm D}_{T+h}$ and $\widehat Z^{\mathrm C}_{T+h}$, so \eqref{main-eq:idx-pythagoras} holds. (ii) The first claim follows from (i); if $\widehat Z^{*}_{T+h}$ is $\mathcal F^{Z}_T$-measurable, the tower property gives $\widehat Z^{\mathrm D}_{T+h}=\mathbb E[\widehat Z^{*}_{T+h}\mid\mathcal F^{Z}_T]=\widehat Z^{*}_{T+h}$. (iii) Since $w+(1-w)=1$, $\kappa_h+\sum_jc_{h,j}Z_{T-j}(w)=w(\kappa_h+\sum_jc_{h,j}U_{D,T-j})+(1-w)(\kappa_h+\sum_jc_{h,j}U_{S,T-j})$. (iv) $z\mapsto100e^{z}$ is strictly increasing; the inequality is the conditional Jensen inequality.\hfill{\setlength{\fboxsep}{0pt}\fbox{\rule{0pt}{1.3ex}\hspace{1.3ex}}}
+\par
+
+\par\medskip\noindent\textsc{Proof of Proposition~\ref{main-prop:idx-bootstrap}.}\ 
+(i) Given the data, the $N$ indicators are i.i.d.\ Bernoulli with variance at most $1/4$; the quantile statement follows from the Glivenko--Cantelli theorem and [S1, Lemma~21.2]. (ii) The stability event is $\{(U_D,U_S)\in\Gamma\}$, $\Gamma=\{F_{T+h}(a)F_{T+h}(1-a)>0\}$, whose boundary lies in the two lines; the portmanteau theorem along almost surely convergent subsequences gives convergence of its bootstrap probability. By the continuous mapping theorem, the bootstrap law of $I^{*}_{T+h}(w)$ converges weakly in probability, and the population quantile statement follows from [S1, Lemma~21.2]. For empirical endpoints, the additional vanishing Monte Carlo error assumption transfers this limit; continuity of the target conditional distribution function at both endpoints then gives limiting coverage $1-\alpha$.\hfill{\setlength{\fboxsep}{0pt}\fbox{\rule{0pt}{1.3ex}\hspace{1.3ex}}}
+\par
